rceName": "Alvise Cadamosto", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "atlantic-slave-trade", "targetName": "Atlantic Slave Trade", "context": "Alvise Cadamosto's voyages and reports transmitted knowledge of West African populations and resources, directly contributing to the development and expansion of the Atlantic slave trade in the mid-15th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "alvise-cadamosto", "sourceName": "Alvise Cadamosto", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "navigazione-di-alvise-cadamosto", "targetName": "Navigazione di Alvise Cadamosto", "context": "Alvise Cadamosto created the seminal travel narrative *Navigazione di Alvise Cadamosto*, which detailed his 1455 and 1456 voyages and provided invaluable geographical and ethnographic information."}], "places": [{"name": "Venice, Italy", "role": "Birthplace; mercantile background"}, {"name": "Lagos, Portugal", "role": "Departure point for voyages"}, {"name": "Gambia River, West Africa", "role": "Key point of exploration"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Alvise Cadamosto", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "alvise-cadamosto"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q360073", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alvise_Cadamosto"], "tags": ["Explorers & Navigators", "cultural-diffusion", "environmental-history", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Alvise Cadamosto's voyages significantly expanded European knowledge of the West African coast, directly informing subsequent Portuguese exploration and solidifying the economic basis for the transatlantic slave trade. His detailed accounts became foundational texts for European understanding of a previously unknown region.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-28T08:19:27Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46124639210", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Alvise Cadamosto was a Venetian explorer and slave trader, who was hired by the Portuguese prince Henry the Navigator and undertook two known journeys to West Africa in 1455 and 1456, accompanied by the Genoese captain Antoniotto Usodimare. Some have credited Cadamosto and his companions with the discovery of the Cape Verde Islands and the points along the Guinea coast from the Gambia River to the Geba River, the greatest leap in the Henrican discoveries since 1446. Cadamosto's accounts of his journeys, including his detailed observations of West African societies, have proven invaluable to historians.", "newValue": "Alvise Cadamosto, a Venetian navigator born around 1432, emerged as a pivotal figure in the early Portuguese maritime expansion, undertaking two groundbreaking voyages to West Africa in 1455 and 1456 under the patronage of Prince Henry the Navigator. His expeditions significantly extended the known European reach along the African coast, documenting new territories, peoples, and commercial opportunities, including the burgeoning trade in enslaved Africans. Cadamosto’s detailed accounts provided an unprecedented glimpse into West African societies and geography, making him a crucial chronicler of the nascent Age of Discovery.

Between 1455 and 1456, Cadamosto, often accompanied by the Genoese captain Antoniotto Usodimare, sailed from Lagos, Portugal. His first voyage in 1455 navigated past the Senegal River and explored the Gambia River, establishing contact with local rulers and engaging in trade for gold, ivory, and enslaved people. The subsequent expedition in 1456 pushed even further south, possibly reaching the Geba River in present-day Guinea-Bissau. During this second journey, Cadamosto and his crew are credited with the discovery of several islands in the Cape Verde archipelago, marking a significant geographical achievement and the greatest leap in Portuguese exploration since 1446.

Cadamosto's most enduring contribution was his meticulously detailed narrative, *Navigazione di Alvise Cadamosto*, published posthumously. This work became an indispensable resource for European cartographers, merchants, and subsequent explorers, offering invaluable insights into the hydrography, ethnography, and commercial potential of West Africa. His reports not only fueled further Portuguese expansion along the African coast but also solidified the economic rationale for the Atlantic slave trade, influencing the strategic understanding that would facilitate future voyages around the Cape of Good Hope and ultimately reshape global commerce and demographics."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-28T08:19:27Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46124639210", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1432, Venice"}, {"title": "Curiosity about unknown territories and navigation advances", "type": "Idea", "year": "1432, Venice"}], "newValue": ["Prince Henry the Navigator's ambition to find new trade routes and sources of wealth drove Portuguese maritime exploration.", "The established Venetian maritime and mercantile expertise provided skilled navigators like Cadamosto for hire by other European powers.", "The economic incentives of the burgeoning Atlantic trade, particularly in gold, ivory, and enslaved people, motivated perilous voyages of discovery."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-28T08:19:27Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#46124639210", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Expanded geographic knowledge and cultural exchange", "type": "Idea", "year": "1488-07-25"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1488-07-25"}], "newValue": ["Cadamosto's voyages significantly extended European geographical knowledge of the